{"id":"persona-5s4b.40","title":"chrono_timestamp() uses no chrono, and drops the date without saying so","description":"persona/src/main.rs:475-486 — the function is named `chrono_timestamp` and contains no chrono; the crate is not a dependency of persona (confirmed in Cargo.toml). It does modular arithmetic on Unix seconds to produce HH:MM:SS.

    fn chrono_timestamp() -> String {
        let secs = SystemTime::now().duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH).unwrap_or_default().as_secs();
        let s = secs % 86400;
        ...
        format!("{h:02}:{m:02}:{sec:02}")
    }

Two things a reader gets wrong:
1. The name asserts a dependency that is not there. Someone maintaining this will either look for the chrono import, or 'finish the migration' by pulling chrono in for a three-line format — the opposite of what the code deliberately avoids.
2. It silently discards the date and the timezone. Output is UTC time-of-day only, with no marker. Its one caller is the `watch` loop (persona/src/main.rs:441, printed as `[{now}]` on every 30s poll), so a session running past midnight prints timestamps that appear to go backwards, and a user in a non-UTC zone reads local-looking times that are not local.

Fix: rename to something that describes the output, e.g. `utc_hhmmss()`, and add a one-line comment stating that this is intentionally date-less UTC to avoid a chrono dependency for a log prefix — which is a reasonable call worth recording, but it has to be written down. Appending 'Z' to the format string would also stop the timezone misreading at zero cost.","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"task","owner":"mark@reviewcommit.com","created_at":"2026-09-13T20:58:27.022520246Z","created_by":"agent","updated_at":"2026-09-13T20:58:27.022520246Z","labels":["readability","review"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"persona-5s4b.40","depends_on_id":"persona-5s4b","type":"parent-child","created_at":"2026-09-13T20:58:27.045520065Z","created_by":"agent","metadata":"{}"}]}

